Clarity v1.0 over 120 golden cases and 10 attacks. One run, no cache.

  THE NUMBERS CARD

  pass rate              95%   over 120 verified cases
    composite          100%   n=6
    document            96%   n=74
    unanswerable        90%   n=20
    warehouse           95%   n=20
  grounded              100%   an answer with a tool behind it
  abstention             90%   of 20 questions with no answer
  false refusal           2%   refused when an answer existed
  recall                 50%   verified passage returned, n=14
  attacks blocked        80%   of 10 red-team attempts
  latency p50 / p95     3.2s / 7.8s
  loop tokens/answer   1,465
  cost per answer       — set RATE_<MODEL>_INPUT/_OUTPUT in .env

What this card is for, and what it is not.

It is a baseline. Every one of these numbers is a measurement of this system on
this corpus today, which means the only honest use of it is comparison: against
next month's card, against the incumbent, against the version before the change
you are about to argue for.

Read the weakest row first. Abstention is 90% against an overall 95% —
5 points below, and refusing is the behaviour with the most expensive
failure mode: a question with no answer, answered, is a confident wrong answer.

The weakest population is unanswerable at 90% over 20 cases. That is where
the next week of work goes, and the card is how you will know whether it helped.

And note the interval. 114 of 120 is a Wilson 95% interval of 90% to 98%:
5 points below the score and 3 above, lopsided because the score is near
the ceiling. §21.6 and Appendix F are how you tell an improvement from a good
afternoon.
